Proof move: $\sum_{k=n}^{\infty}\frac{x^k}{k}$ converges uniformly on $[0,1]$, thus squaring and integrating term by term; $\lim_{n\to\infty} n\int_{x=0}^{1}\left(\sum_{k=n}^{\infty}\frac{x^k}{k}\right)^2 f(x)\,dx = \lim_{n\to\infty} n\int_{x=0}^{1}\left(\sum_{k=n}^{\infty}\frac{x^k}{k}\right)^2 f(x)\,dx$ simplifies to $\lim_{n\to\infty} n\int_{x=0}^{1}\left(\sum_{k=n}^{\infty}\frac{x^k}{k}\right)^2 f(x)\,dx$;
Key insight: Exploiting uniform convergence allows treating the series as a constant

